{% set local_problem %}
<p>Lake Guntersville creates a perfect storm for gutter problems: heavy tree coverage drops debris constantly, and the humidity turns that debris into something worse than leaves.</p>

<p>In a dry climate, leaves in gutters eventually blow out or come out easily when cleaned. Near the lake, that debris absorbs moisture from day one. It compacts. It decomposes. Within a few months, you don't have leaves anymore — you have a dense organic layer that holds water against the metal, weighs down the gutters, and becomes nearly impossible to remove without serious effort.</p>

<p>We cleaned a house on <a href="/recent-projects/">Point of Pines</a> that had gone two years without maintenance. The debris wasn't recognizable as leaves anymore — it had basically become soil. Things growing in it. We had to chisel sections out. What should have been a $175 cleaning turned into a $400 project because of what the humidity had done to normal debris.</p>

<p>For full-time residents, that means 3-4 cleanings per year at $150-200 each: $600-800 annually just to maintain gutters.</p>

<p>For vacation owners and rental properties, it's worse. You're not there to notice when gutters fill up. Debris accumulates for months. Clogs develop. Overflow starts. By the time you visit — or worse, by the time a renter calls with water coming in somewhere — you've got damage that costs real money to fix.</p>

<p>Gutter guards break that cycle. Debris lands on top, dries out, blows or washes off. Your gutters stay clear and functional whether you're checking them weekly or visiting twice a year.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>Gutter guards are covers that fit over or into your existing gutters. They let water through while blocking debris. Simple concept, but execution matters — especially in humid environments where the wrong guards fail fast.</p>

<p><strong>Foam guards</strong> are sponge inserts that fill the gutter cavity. They're cheap and easy to install. They're also terrible near the lake. The foam absorbs humidity constantly, never fully dries, and starts breaking down within 18-24 months. We've pulled foam guards off <a href="/recent-projects/">Guntersville</a> homes that had turned to mush — literally falling apart in our hands. Don't waste your money.</p>

<p><strong>Screen guards</strong> are mesh or perforated covers. Better than foam, but fine debris falls through and accumulates anyway. Pine needles, seed pods, shingle grit — all pass through standard screens. In lake humidity, that fine debris becomes the same sludge problem, just slower.</p>

<p><strong>Micro-mesh guards</strong> have openings around 50-100 microns — small enough to block everything including pine needles and roof grit. Water passes through via surface tension. Debris lands on top, dries, and blows or washes off. This is what we install on lake properties. Costs more. Actually works.</p>

<p><strong>Reverse-curve (solid top) guards</strong> use surface tension to pull water into a slot while debris falls off the edge. Works well for large leaves, less effective for pine needles which can stick to the curve. Mixed results in our experience.</p>

<p>For Guntersville's mix of pine and hardwood debris plus the humidity factor, micro-mesh is the answer. The premium is worth it for guards that actually perform long-term.</p>
{% endset %}

